{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01KutYU1ngPc841mXm43gks1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Here's what was written for **Q11 — Convert implicit user behavior (edits, acceptance) into training signals**:\n\n---\n\n**Key content in `04-011-...md`:**\n\n- **Signal taxonomy** — acceptance (strong positive), dismissal/regeneration (clean negative), and the noisy middle: light edits (<20% token change = moderate positive), heavy edits (>50% = rejected original, chosen = user's final version). Also: clipboard copy, downstream engagement (commit/publish).\n\n- **Noise-reduction pipeline** — MinHash dedup by prompt hash, confidence weighting per signal type, edit-distance bounds (10–80% for edit-reconstruction pairs), 1–2% human audit to catch adversarial edits.\n\n- **Preference pair construction** — DPO format `{prompt, chosen, rejected}`: clean acceptances vs. dismissals, or original vs. user-edited version for the edit-reconstruction case.\n\n- **Training integration** — DPO as the default (no separate reward model, uses `trl.DPOTrainer`/Axolotl on a LoRA adapter); RLHF/PPO reserved for multi-objective reward scenarios.\n\n- **Feedback cadence** — weekly LoRA DPO fine-tune → shadow eval on golden holdout → canary 5% traffic → promote on ≥55% win rate.\n\n- **Critical pitfall** — selection bias: users who accept suggestions ≠ random sample; mitigate by stratifying pairs by user cohort and up-weighting expert signals.\n\n**Next up in plan:** Q12 — Quantization — tradeoffs between size, speed, accuracy?"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":335,"cache_read_input_tokens":65625,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":335,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0d4cd77d-1a72-427d-8f4c-9494d5b99242","uuid":"f4a0cd60-f7f4-4d86-b117-ba341fbb2ab4"}
